# Authentication and Authorization

## Library
No auth. Pure functions. Caller is responsible for credentials.

## Server — JWT
Via `fastapi-users` (or equivalent). User identity in `sub` claim, tenant in `tenant` claim, role in `role` claim.

Auth middleware: `apps/server/src/dqt_server/middleware/auth.py`
- Decodes JWT on every request.
- Populates `request.state.user_id`, `request.state.tenant_id`, `request.state.role`.

## Roles
| Role | Description |
|---|---|
| `viewer` | Read-only access to all resources |
| `editor` | Create/update checks, acknowledge incidents |
| `admin` | Full access, manage users, configure integrations |
| `oncall` | Can manage incidents and tasks |
| `sysadmin` | Cross-tenant access, act-as emulation |

Permissions defined in `apps/server/src/dqt_server/auth/permissions.py`. Map role → allowed (resource, action) pairs.

## Sysadmin emulation
Sysadmins can act-as another user (`X-Act-As: <user_id>` header). Logged in audit log with both identities (`actor_id` = sysadmin, `impersonating_id` = target user).

## Routes
```
POST /auth/register    # new user + tenant
POST /auth/login       # returns JWT
GET  /auth/me          # current user profile
POST /auth/logout      # invalidate session token
```

## Session tokens
`user_sessions (id, user_id, token_hash, expires_at)` — token stored as bcrypt hash, never plaintext.

## Testing auth
Dependency-override `get_current_user` with a fixture user. **Never patch middleware.** Never hardcode a test JWT secret.
```python
app.dependency_overrides[get_current_user] = lambda: fixture_user
```

## Multi-tenant scoping
All queries pass through `TenantScopedRepository` which injects `tenant_id` automatically. RLS in Postgres as defense-in-depth. See `database.mdc`.

## Password policy
bcrypt, min 8 chars. No other policy enforced in MVP.
